To diagnose appendicitis, medical professionals often perform a combination of physical exams, medical history reviews, and diagnostic tests. The exams may include a gentle abdominal palpation to assess tenderness, particularly in the lower right quadrant, and a rectal exam to check for tenderness in the rectum. 

Diagnostic tests might encompass complete blood counts (CBC) to look for signs of infection, blood tests to check for elevated white blood cell counts, and urinalysis to rule out urinary tract infections. Imaging tests such as an abdominal X-ray, ultrasound, or CT scan may be ordered to visualize the appendix and surrounding tissues. 

Specific symptoms that might distinguish appendicitis from other issues like ovarian cysts or gastrointestinal problems include the location and nature of the pain (appendicitis typically presents with pain that initially might be diffuse or around the navel and then localizes to the lower right abdomen), the presence of rebound tenderness (pain upon pressing and then releasing the abdomen), and the progression of symptoms over time. However, these distinctions are not definitive and require a professional medical evaluation for accurate diagnosis.

It's also worth noting that while ovarian cysts can cause similar pain, they are more likely to cause pelvic pain and might be associated with menstrual irregularities or other gynecological symptoms. Gastrointestinal issues, on the other hand, might cause more diffuse abdominal pain and could be accompanied by changes in bowel habits or vomiting.

Given your symptoms, it remains crucial to seek medical attention to determine the exact cause of your abdominal pain and to receive appropriate treatment. The medical professional will be able to provide a more detailed assessment and guide the next steps based on the results of the exams and tests.

